  "failure_statement": "The agent began or continued work in an incorrect environment — a stale branch, a default/cached directory, or the wrong workspace path — instead of the specific branch or workspace the user had named, without first checking current state (pwd/branch) against the user's instruction.",
  "remedy_content": {
    "target": "global",
    "text": "# Verify Working Environment Before Acting\n\nWhen a user names a specific branch or workspace path (e.g. \"use /work/foo\", \"on the release branch\"), confirm your current state matches it (`pwd`, `git branch --show-current`, etc.) before making changes. If it doesn't match, switch to the named one or ask — never proceed on a stale, cached, or default environment and assume it's correct."
  },
  "remedy_type": "claude-md",
  "routing_rationale": "All five promoted incidents (harbor#16, lantern#22/23, aurora#6/7) share one root cause: the agent acted (or claimed to act) using the wrong branch, directory, or workspace path instead of the one the user explicitly named (\"stale fake branch\", \"/work/fake-lantern workspace\", \"wrong fake environment\"). This is a single failure mode, not heterogeneous evidence, so no split is warranted. A hook is not viable because there is no generic mechanical check for \"is this the environment the user meant\" — the correct branch/workspace name is contextual to each request, not a fixed pattern a script can verify. It is not procedural/multi-step enough to justify a skill (no branching logic, tool sequence, or multi-step workflow — just a habit of checking state before acting). A short standing instruction to confirm current working context (pwd/branch/workspace) against what the user specified, before making changes, is the cheapest fix and directly targets the failure. None of the three existing remedies address environment/workspace verification (R1 and R2 are about verifying completion claims via test/release commands; R3 is about unauthorized destructive deletion despite a stated constraint), so this is a new global rule, not a revision.",
  "singleton": false
